{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786993884,"nonce":"+reWVMC6BALx34uFlAES+Z9Ndw8=","title":"The authoritative entity is AlertOccurrence and every surface leads with Alert","message":"**What is wrong.** The entity that owns oto's state machine is named for its position in a\ndata model, and no surface presents it as primary. `Occurrence` is accurate and says nothing\nabout what a person does with it.\n\n**The demonstration.** `docs/design/SPEC.md` §B states it outright: \"The authoritative machine\nruns on **AlertOccurrence**. `Alert.state` and `AlertGroup.state` are projections.\" §B.1 puts\nboth axes there — `occurrence.state` owned by ingestion and the reconciler,\n`occurrence.ack_state` owned by the API. SPEC:90 adds \"This is what you acknowledge and whose\n**firing duration** is measured.\"\n\nThe schema agrees and the alert does not. `alert_occurrences`\n(`db/migrations/00007_alerts.sql:119-208`) carries `ack_state`, `acked_by`, `acked_by_label`,\n`acked_at` and `ack_note` under four CHECKs — `occ_ack_ck`, `occ_acklabel_ck`,\n`occ_ackorder_ck`, `occ_acknote_ck` — plus `rule_snapshot_id`, `reopen_of`, `resolve_reason`\nand both clock pairs. `alerts.ack_state` (`:42`) is a bare enum with no actor and no\ntimestamp. `occ_one_open_idx` guarantees at most one open per alert and `occ_seq_uniq` makes\n`seq` gapless, so \"the 12th firing\" is answerable — and ADR 0024 records that occurrences\nsurvive retention while `alert_events` does not.\n\nADR 0003's rejected alternative names the gap directly: collapsing occurrence into event would\nleave \"no natural key for *the thing this Slack thread is about*.\"\n\n**Why it matters.** ADR 0003 opens with \"'Alert' is overloaded to uselessness\" and splits three\nentities to fix it; §A.1 bans `alert` used to mean an occurrence for the same reason. The\nproduct's differentiator is the replayable episode — start, end, ack, outcome, rule snapshot —\nand it is the entity a reader meets last, behind a name that describes storage rather than use.\nA name that does not carry its meaning decays into the wrong one, which is the premise the\nwhole vocabulary section rests on.\n\n`case` sits adjacent to §A.1's scope bans (`incident`, `triage`, `assignee`, `owner`,\n`responder`), and §A.1's rule is that such a word is \"presumed over the line until argued\notherwise against FR-1 **by name**\". So the argument is part of the work. The line that makes\n`case` legitimate is H-2: delete every alert, and if the object still means anything it is an\nincident and it is out. A Case is meaningless without its alert, because it *is* that alert's\nfiring span. `correlation` (SPEC:3624, DEFERRED-POST-V1) remains the name for anything tying\ndisjoint signals, and nothing here pulls it forward.\n\n**Where.** `db/migrations/00007_alerts.sql:119-208`; `internal/alerts/domain/` (`Occurrence`,\nand the ten `EventOccurrence*` constants at `event.go:66-80`); `internal/alerts/service/`;\n`internal/notification/domain/idempotency.go:19` (`SubjectKind`);\n`internal/notification/repository/rollup.go:37` (`RollupOccurrence`);\n`internal/notification/repository/notifications.go:70` (`occurrence_id`);\n`internal/grouping/` (`alert_group_members.occurrence_id`); `api/openapi/openapi.yaml`;\n`web/src/`; `docs/design/SPEC.md:90`; `tools/lintvocab`; `test/arch/sqltables_test.go`.\n\n**Scope.** large\n\n**Done when.** An ADR records the FR-1-by-name argument for `case` and the Case/correlation\nline, and ADR 0013 references it. `alert_occurrences` is `alert_cases`, `occurrence_id` is\n`case_id`, the ten `occurrence.*` event types are `case.*`, dedupe keys read\n`case:{id}:opened`, `RollupOccurrence` is `RollupCase`, and `occurrence` appears in no Go\nidentifier, JSON field, column name or UI string — the closing condition SPEC §P-5 used for\nthe `escalation` rename, reused verbatim. `tools/lintvocab` gains `occurrence` with an\nallowlist for `docs/` and the new ADR, and `test/arch/sqltables_test.go` names `alert_cases`.\nNo column is named `case`, which is reserved in both Go and SQL; the table and the type are\nnot affected by that.","files":null}]}